            <ArticleTitle>Analgesia in newborns: a case-control study of the efficacy of nutritive and non-nutritive sucking stimuli.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>365-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2317-17822013005000002</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To verify the nutritive and non-nutritive stimuli efficacy in the newborn's response to pain during venipuncture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The main sample was composed of 64 newborns that were randomly divided into three groups. The first group (n=20) received nutritive sucking stimulus that was performed through maternal breastfeeding. The second group (n=21) received non-nutritive sucking stimulus that was performed through the introduction of the researcher little finger in the newborn's oral cavity. The third group or control group (n=23) did not receive any analgesia stimulus. The newborns were evaluated using the Neonatal Infant Pain Scale, and the responses to painful stimuli were compared.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The nutritive as well as non-nutritive suction methods provided a comforting effect, resulting in lower pain response scores (p&lt;0.05). There was no difference between the analgesia provided by both methods (p&gt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The nutritive and the non-nutritive sucking stimuli proved to be efficacious tools in relieving pain among newborns.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
